\[N_{NPE}^{rec}=N_{inc}\times purity-N_{PE}^{rec}/\varepsilon_{PE} \tag{3.5.4}\]

### 3.6 Heavy quarkonia decay and Drell-Yan

Before we proceed to detailed discussions of simulation studies of efficiencies including \(\varepsilon_{PE}\), we here discuss some other sources contributing to the electrons. The second largest background, other than the photonic electrons discussed above, is the electrons from heavy quarkonia, _i.e_ J/\(\Psi\) and \(\Upsilon\) decays and Drell-Yan processes. While heavy quarkonia also consist of heavy quarks, their energy loss mechanisms are different from single heavy quarks, and model calculations have to treat them separately. Electrons from J/\(\Psi\) and \(\Upsilon\) decays should be subtracted from the final non-photonic electrons, in both _p_+_p_ and Au+Au collisions. The Drell-Yan processes should also be subtracted since they are initial state processes and not affected by the QGP, although they could be affected by cold nuclear effects.

