## Appendix B Error propagation in elliptic flow calculations

The Standard Deviation (or "error") of a measured variable is the square root of the variance of the sample mean for this variable, _i.e._

\[S=\frac{RMS}{\sqrt{N}}=\frac{\sqrt{\frac{\sum(x_{i}-\hat{\mu})^{2}}{N}}}{\sqrt{N}}\] (B.0.1)

where root mean square (RMS)\(=\sqrt{\frac{\sum(x_{i}-\hat{\mu})^{2}}{N}}\) is used.

To combine several data sets

\[S^{2}_{total}=\frac{\frac{\sum_{\alpha}[\sum_{i}(x_{\alpha i}-\hat{\mu}_{ \alpha})^{2}+N_{\alpha}\hat{\mu}_{\alpha}^{2}]}{\sum_{\alpha}N_{\alpha}}-[ \frac{\sum_{\alpha}(N_{\alpha}\hat{\mu}_{\alpha})}{\sum_{\alpha}N_{\alpha}}]^{ 2}}{\sum_{\alpha}N_{\alpha}}\] (B.0.2)

where \(\alpha\) means different data sets, _e.g._ different \(\eta\) bins or \(p_{T}\) bins.

We can check this with, for example, the case of two data sets, where

\[\hat{\mu}=\frac{N_{1}\hat{\mu}_{1}+N_{2}\hat{\mu}_{2}}{N_{1}+N_{2}}\]

